  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 400 PM EST SUN NOV 8 2026\n\nA split flow pattern will still be in place across the CONUS at the \nstart of the period, with the polar jet stream generally over the \nnorthern edge of the CONUS and the subtropical jet stream across \nnorthern Mexico and the Gulf Coast. At the surface, high \npressure/ridging from east TX to the central Appalachians will be \nproviding us with continued dry weather. With abundant daytime \nsunshine and a milder air mass having arrived, temperatures will \nreturn to near or a little above normal readings for Tuesday and \nWednesday. However, during this time the makings of our next change \nwill be setting up. \n\nAs of Tuesday morning, an upper level shortwave trough will be \ncrossing the Rockies near the Canadian border. Further east, a \nlarger scale upper trough with embedded upper lows will be over \neastern Canada. The shortwave will be picked up by the eastern \nCanadian trough and a transient but unusually deep and amplified \ntrough should develop over and sweep through the eastern CONUS, \nfurther aided by unification with the southern/subtropical stream. \nAt its peak, models suggest a nearly 200 mph upper jet off the \nnortheast coast during the weekend. Needless to say, a powerful \nsurface low is forecast to develop with this system. \n\nThere is still variation among models, both inter-model and run to \nrun. Differences include the timing and track of the surface low and \nhow early/how far west it begins to rapidly intensify, and the \nmoisture pattern on the back side of the low as it departs our area. \nA model blend suggests the early strengthening low will track near \nus or a little to our north, with the bulk of our precip coming on \nWednesday night and Thursday as the low approaches and passes by, \nOn the back side of the system there is a potential for light precip \nto linger at least into Friday night in the upslope flow and \npossible low level moisture connection to Lake Michigan. With enough \ncold air showing up in some runs, what precip that remains could mix \nwith or change to snow Thursday night or Friday, but light \naccumulations (if any) are still likely to be confined to the \nhighest elevations near the VA border.",
